# The composed remainder, computed two ways. One row per composed layer, with the figure pivoted
# through its parts beside the one derived from its totals, and whether they agree.
#
# The disagreements are the spread conversions
#   The layers that disagree are exactly those whose remainder walk passes a conversion factor with
#   width, and `spread` says which those are. Every other composed layer converts at one or at a
#   point value, so there is no correlation to remove and the figures agree exactly; those are the
#   controls, and without them a difference here would prove nothing. algebra/composed_remainder
#   holds both halves on every run. Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry `composed_remainder`.
#
# Disagreement does not mean a document is wrong
#   layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc differences the composed totals, and one conversion factor
#   multiplied both of them, so its figure counts that factor's spread twice. The pivot converts
#   each part's own remainder, so the factor appears once per term. Both are arithmetically
#   correct; only the pivot is the remainder, and layers/remainder.sqlc carries it. This section
#   is a referral, not a violation.
-- composition/fused_remainders.sqlc, with the two figures set against each other.
SELECT f.composition                                             AS "composition!",
       f.composed_layer                                          AS "composed_layer!",
       f.parts                                                   AS "parts!",
       f.unit                                                    AS "unit!",
       f.pivoted_low::float8                                     AS "pivoted_low!",
       f.pivoted_mode::float8                                    AS "pivoted_mode!",
       f.pivoted_high::float8                                    AS "pivoted_high!",
       f.derived_low::float8                                     AS "derived_low!",
       f.derived_mode::float8                                    AS "derived_mode!",
       f.derived_high::float8                                    AS "derived_high!",
       f.spread                                                  AS "spread!",
       (f.pivoted_low = f.derived_low
    AND f.pivoted_mode = f.derived_mode
    AND f.pivoted_high = f.derived_high)                         AS "agrees!"
FROM (
    :compose(composition/fused_remainders.sqlc)
) f
ORDER BY "agrees!", f.composition, f.composed_layer
